WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:06.670
Speaker 1: We're going to continue our conversations
with 2022 midterm candidates today, we will

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:07.880
be speaking with J.R.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:13.240
Majnu Sky, who's the Republican candidate
for Congress hoping to represent Ohio's 9th

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.240
District.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.240
J.R.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.240
It's really great to have you on today.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.579
Speaker 2: Thanks, Dave.

00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:18.579
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:18.579 --> 00:00:19.579
I appreciate it.

00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:21.179
Speaker 1: So so why are you running?

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:22.640
What led you to run?

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:29.649
Speaker 2: Well, you know, I lived in the
Ninth District for pretty much my entire life.

00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:35.520
Born and raised here and I become more and
more active in the community.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.920
And you know, I decided that with with Marcy
Kaptur being at, you know, having served office

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:47.400
for 40 years and you know, just the way the
country, I think is going it at this time,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.520
I think that it's time.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.630
It was time for someone like me to jump in
the race and some of those actually born here

00:00:53.630 --> 00:01:00.630
and that was raised here understands the district,
you know, grew up and lived through all the

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.480
trials and tribulations that the district
has to offer.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.480
And so I'm going to serve the country.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.740
I think that, you know, I could be a an asset
to all the people that live here.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:15.060
Speaker 1: What things would you say are not
going in the right direction in the district?

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.020
Speaker 2: Representation, I think, is one
thing.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.880
You know, if you if you go across District
nine and talk to folks, regardless of their

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.850
political affiliation, they find many people
don't even know who Marcy is.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.960
And, you know, having been in office for 40
years, I find that troubling.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.700
And secondly, we're becoming more and more
of a polarized nation.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.130
And I think getting back into the practice
of engaging in healthy political discourse

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.320
is missing.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:52.369
And you know, that's something that that I've
always been about, and I really think that

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:58.179
we need someone to bring that behavior and
that mindset into the leadership position

00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:00.999
of a representative for this district.

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:06.399
Speaker 1: One of the things that your I believe
it's your communications director, it was

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:12.470
your communications contact that we were in
touch with, said, was that you're a constitutional

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.640
warrior and you want to take the country back.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.310
Can you talk about who do you want to take
it back from?

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:24.010
Speaker 2: I think it needs to be taken back
from the establishment from from those that

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.290
were elected by the people to fulfill, you
know, various roles of office.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.930
But I think the people are.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:38.360
The general consensus, in my opinion, is that
the people feel as if the government and our

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.920
elected representatives are extremely different
from what our expectations were when we voted

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.920
them into office.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.490
Speaker 1: Can you give an example of that?

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.130
In other words, what would be who would be
an individual where we would say the expectation

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.230
was a and here's B, which is what they're
actually doing and we don't like it.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:03:01.080
Speaker 2: I would say example a would be
Marcy Kaptur.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.700
You would expect if you made a phone call
to your representative that you would get

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.170
contacted and be contacted or responded to
within a reasonable amount of time.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:15.660
And I very rarely have met anyone that can
describe to me a situation where they've called

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.170
and they've got a response within 72, 48,
48, 72 hours.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.690
I mean, some folks do not even get responded
back to.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.690
Speaker 1: So one of your big things would
be, I'm hearing you say, I think I'm hearing

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.520
you say you would set up a system to respond
to constituents more quickly?

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.540
Speaker 2: Absolutely.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.640
One of my campaign commitments, actually my
first one, is that, you know, I hold a town

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.030
hall in every county within the district that
I represent at least once a quarter

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:46.120
Speaker 1: when it comes to the constitutional
piece of some of the material that we got

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.120
from you.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.300
Are there parts of the Constitution that you
believe aren't currently being followed?

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.590
Speaker 2: Yeah, I think free speech is being
trampled on right now.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:04:00.620
And, you know, from from various outlets,
mostly from what I what I see is social media

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.780
and Big Tech.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.970
OK, I can give examples of that.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.570
Speaker 1: Now, just to be clear, J.R.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:10.900
So our audience understands I when I said
the Constitution, when you say free speech.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.819
Am I right to understand that you mean the
First Amendment to the Constitution?

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:16.040
Speaker 2: Yeah, our First Amendment.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.340
Speaker 1: OK, right now, the institutions
you mentioned aren't governmental institutions,

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.840
and the First Amendment only applies to government.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.100
So in what way would that apply to Big Tech
or social media?

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.220
Speaker 2: Well, generally speaking, I think
we have a right to free speech and when we

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:41.480
have institutions that are meant to and intended
to be a forum where public discourse can can

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:48.160
take place and you have arbitrary decision
makers that decide what is good and what's

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.610
bad, I think that's that is, in my opinion,
an assault against our First Amendment rights.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:57.290
Speaker 1: So let's dig into that because
I think this is such a media issue where there's

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.130
so much to discuss.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.540
Will you concede that the First Amendment
only applies to government like Twitter has

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.540
nothing.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.720
Twitter is not governed by the First Amendment.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.980
I hear you saying maybe you would like it
to be, but do you agree that as far as the

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.000
law is today?

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.350
Twitter in the First Amendment are not intertwined
in any way.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:17.750
Speaker 2: I would say that.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.430
I'm not I wouldn't be prepared to answer that.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.770
Now, in the manner which I would like, I like
to look into things a little bit more.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:32.050
But I would say that the general perception
is reality right in at this day and age, I

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.350
feel as if there is.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:41.870
Again, I think when I post something on Facebook
and it gets taken down and it's labeled as

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:43.500
promoting hate speech.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.690
When I'm just posting a picture of a meme
of someone, there's no violence and it's just

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:47.920
someone's face, OK?

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.500
I don't I don't comprehend how that is, you
know, promoting violence.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.490
Or it's to me
Speaker 1: that sounds like that's your opinion

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:59.440
about a meme, but I guess so we can establish
a new set of facts on which we can then talk

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.630
about this.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:05.201
Facebook Having terms of service has nothing
to do with the First Amendment to the Constitution

00:06:05.201 --> 00:06:07.220
and government abridging speech, right?

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.420
You can agree with that.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:14.490
So it sounds like you don't like the way some
social media companies are enforcing terms

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:15.490
of service.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.490
Is that fair?

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:17.490
Speaker 2: Yeah, that's fair.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:25.610
Speaker 1: In general, as a conservative constitutional
conservative, do you favor more or less business

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:26.610
regulation?

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:32.370
Speaker 2: I'm less I would think, but it's
all it's all circumstantial.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.280
I mean, you know, there has to be some regulations.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.170
Not all of our businesses are automatically
ethical and principally driven.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.100
So 100 percent,
Speaker 1: 100 percent, but in general, you

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:46.940
tend to favor letting businesses do what they
want to do as long as it's not against the

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:47.940
law.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:48.940
Maybe.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.990
Is that fair
Speaker 2: to a degree, but I mean, you know,

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:58.699
some some you know, some some businesses work
under the auspices of purchase agreements.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:01.340
That's not necessarily law labor relations
agreements.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:07.380
So I mean, you know, there's just a fine line
of of being ethical and, you know, meaning,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:12.300
excuse me, meeting their their responsibilities
regardless of where that where that responsibility

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.150
stems from
Speaker 1: a 100 percent fair.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.389
I'm with you all the way as applied.

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:17.990
Speaker 2: Oh yeah, go ahead.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.430
I'm actually more of a libertarian.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.430
Speaker 1: Oh, so even more interesting then.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:29.430
So as a libertarian, would you agree that
forcing a private company like Twitter or

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:36.300
Facebook to have to publish your meme would
be extremely authoritarian regulation of businesses?

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.462
Speaker 2: I mean, I can agree.

00:07:38.462 --> 00:07:39.462
I can.

00:07:39.462 --> 00:07:40.462
I can.

00:07:40.462 --> 00:07:41.462
I can say yes, but I mean, it's all.

00:07:41.462 --> 00:07:42.462
It's all based on context.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:43.462
You know, when?

00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:44.462
When.

00:07:44.462 --> 00:07:51.710
I mean, there's nothing that explicitly limits
my ability to post that meme because there

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.190
was no violation of their terms of service
as I can.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:57.450
Speaker 1: So, you know, it sounds like you
disagree with the enforcement, but you're

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:01.970
not actually suggesting the government force
Facebook to have to let you publish it.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.270
You just disagree with their choice.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.410
Speaker 2: Right.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:08.430
And I think I think the, you know, the arbitration
needs to be taken out of the picture.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:09.630
It needs to be black and white.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:13.260
There needs to be if they're going to have
rules, those rules should be, you know, enforced.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:14.260
OK.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.639
Speaker 1: You know, I think we might disagree
about how to enforce it, but I don't I don't

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:20.410
disagree with you in principle.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:24.229
What this is just kind of like a gut check
question that I've been asking candidates

00:08:24.229 --> 00:08:25.229
that we talked to.

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:26.710
And shockingly, sometimes they don't.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.419
They're not able to answer who's the current
president of the United States, Chair Joe

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:32.419
Biden.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:33.419
OK.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:34.419
And is he?

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:35.419
Do you think he was elected legitimately?

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:37.760
Speaker 2: I think the election was stolen
personally.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:38.760
Oh, you do.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.320
I think there were extreme irregularities
with with the election.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.320
Yes.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:47.110
Speaker 1: In which states,
Speaker 2: Oh man, you got me on the spot.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.220
I know.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.570
Speaker 1: Well, I guess let me put it a different
way.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:56.580
There is no one state that Biden won.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.300
That's in question that would have flipped
it to Trump.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.150
So I guess what group of states might be part
of it?

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.089
Speaker 2: Well, I mean, Georgia had election
irregularities.

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:10.660
Arizona, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, even here
in Ohio, we had some.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.339
Speaker 1: OK.

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:17.990
And when you say irregularities, there's been
claims levied like, for example, dead people

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:18.990
voted.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:19.990
People voted twice.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:24.830
You know, all these different things, somebody
there were, quote, massive dumps of ballots

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.730
in the middle of the night, this type of thing.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.990
What specifically do you think happened like
in Georgia for?

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:34.700
And I mentioned Georgia because Republicans
oversaw that and were in charge of the election.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:35.700
What do you think happened?

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.930
Speaker 2: I mean, personally, I don't speculate
on those things.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.630
What I say is that, you know, where there's
smoke, there's fire.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.120
OK, I believe that too.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.760
I believe that our Congress had an obligation
to, you know, put put those concerns of a

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:52.779
lot of American people to rest, whether they
were right or wrong.

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:57.970
Our congressional members have an obligation
and the fact that we want, you know, we we've

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:02.770
moved forward and Congress hasn't put those
issues to rest.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:09.160
That is it's just it's exacerbated the problem
where we have people on both sides of the

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.670
aisle that are that are so polarized.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:11.670
I mean.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:16.149
Speaker 1: Well, help me understand putting
to rest because when I do, I try to do a fair

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:17.760
hearing right of the claims.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:18.760
Sure.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:24.710
And we had 63 different state level legal
complaints filed, all found to be either without

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:27.470
merit or to lack standing.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:32.730
And we had a variety of different investigations
take place in different states.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.430
There was one in Georgia, there was one in
Arizona.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.160
Sometimes it turns out Joe Biden won by even
more.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.140
What would satisfy you?

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:46.070
That would quote put it to rest as you say,
Speaker 2: an audit, a full on audit of the

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:52.720
states that that have asked for one, if if
you know, Arizona claims that they've had

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.220
election irregularities and they are the people
there are asking for an audit, I believe that,

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.510
you know, our Congress should allow that to
move forward.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.430
Speaker 1: But they did it, not it in Arizona.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.470
And Biden got even more votes.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:05.470
OK.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.500
The Cyber Ninjas did.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.010
The audit was was that audit not satisfactory?

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.250
Speaker 2: That's true.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:11.250
That's true.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.350
You're right on that.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:14.540
What about Wisconsin, Pennsylvania?

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:20.490
Speaker 1: Yeah, I guess the question would
be if there are irregularities?

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:24.880
Why is it that no courts seem to agree with
that, even when it's Trump appointed judges?

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:25.959
You know, I'm trying to figure it out.

00:11:25.959 --> 00:11:31.029
Speaker 2: Myself, yeah, but I mean, you're
someone of putting a slight on its Republican

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:33.050
this republic, I'm not looking at it in that
manner.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.410
I'm looking at it from the people.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.730
OK, the people are asking for it.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.360
I mean, it's not a Partizan argument.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:43.459
It's if the people believe that the election
was stolen, whether if it's they, you know,

00:11:43.459 --> 00:11:47.490
they believe it in their hearts or, you know,
they're they're out surfing the internet and

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:51.100
they're running rampant on on message boards,
right?

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.160
We have an obligation.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:53.160
In my opinion.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:59.209
We have an obligation as the government to
put those concerns to rest.

00:11:59.209 --> 00:12:01.709
And I, if they were put to rest, people like
me.

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:04.160
I mean, look, I'm I'm a very reasonable guy.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:10.540
Yeah, I might be a Trump supporter, but that
doesn't mean, you know, I'm doing things that

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:14.130
certain folks would attribute to your rampant
Trump.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:17.050
Speaker 1: No, that's why I think this conversation
is so useful.

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:19.760
So I guess my question would be like, what's
the threshold, right?

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:24.530
Because most people in Wisconsin don't think
that it was rigged in Wisconsin or that it

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:25.530
was stolen.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:27.950
There are some people that do, but it's not
the majority.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:32.680
So what should be the threshold to trigger
a multimillion dollar audit at taxpayer expense?

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:35.260
Speaker 2: I think I think it all comes from.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:39.740
I mean, look, each state has has to maintain
their election integrity.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:40.810
Right, right.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:48.630
And so long as each state is satisfied and
the people aren't, I mean, you have thousands

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:52.720
of people that are screaming election fraud.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:53.720
Maybe it's up.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:57.200
Maybe it's around of of of diplomacy.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:58.200
I don't know.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.120
Speaker 1: But no, but that's I mean, that's
what I'm trying to figure out because so you've

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.270
got almost six million people in Wisconsin,
right?

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:09.540
Most people think the election was the results
are the real results Biden won.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:14.180
If only five Wisconsin with Wisconsinites,
I don't know what the word would be if five

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:18.330
Wisconsin residents think it was stolen on
the basis of five people.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.339
Should there be a multimillion dollar audit?

00:13:20.339 --> 00:13:21.720
No, no.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:22.720
OK.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.470
What about 100?

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:25.470
No.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.050
So then I guess what I'm asking is, what is
the threshold?

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:31.410
Speaker 2: I mean, it's not you're asking
me to answer questions that I don't believe

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:32.610
are relevant to.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:33.610
Oh, OK.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:34.610
Yeah.

